      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "For the function $f(x,y)=e^{x} \\ln{(y )}+y x^{7},$ evaluate $\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}$ at the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$ Give an exact answer: {{value}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(e^{x} \\ln{(y )}+y x^{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(e^{x} \\ln{(y )})+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(y x^{7}) \\\\  & =e^{x}\\cdot \\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\ln{(y )})+x^{7}\\cdot \\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(y) \\\\  & =e^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{y})+x^{7}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =\\frac{e^{x}}{y}+x^{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}{|}_{(1,\\frac{1}{2})} & =\\frac{e^{(1)}}{(\\frac{1}{2})}+(1)^{7} \\\\  & =1 + 2 \\cdot e\\end{aligned}\n$$",
